#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:3
msgid "Aligned Instruction Bundling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:6
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:8
msgid ""
"*Aligned instruction bundling* partitions the instructions in a section into "
"fixed-size, naturally aligned groups called bundles, and guarantees that no "
"instruction ever crosses a bundle boundary. Consecutive instructions can "
"also be grouped so that the assembler guarantees the whole group resides "
"within a single bundle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:14
msgid ""
"Bundling is a building block for software-based fault isolation (SFI) and "
"sandboxing schemes. Forcing every instruction to begin at one of a "
"statically known set of offsets gives the instruction stream a single "
"canonical decoding: control flow cannot jump into the middle of an "
"instruction to manufacture a different, unchecked instruction sequence. When "
"combined with masking of indirect branch targets to bundle-aligned "
"addresses, this constrains all control flow to a statically verifiable set "
"of locations and instructions. Bundling is used by the x86-64 implementation "
"of :doc:`Lightweight Fault Isolation (LFI) <LFI>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:26
msgid ""
"The current LLVM implementation supports bundling only for x86 ELF targets. "
"Other targets and object file formats reject the directives below rather "
"than emit unbundled code silently."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:31
msgid "``.bundle_align_mode``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:37
msgid ""
"Enables aligned bundle mode and sets the bundle size to ``2^abs-expr`` "
"bytes, where ``abs-expr`` is a power-of-two exponent between 1 and 30 (as "
"for the ``.p2align`` directive). For example, ``.bundle_align_mode 5`` "
"selects 32-byte bundles. Unlike GNU as, ``.bundle_align_mode 0`` is "
"rejected: bundling cannot be turned off once enabled."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:43
msgid ""
"While bundling is enabled, the assembler ensures that no single instruction "
"spans a boundary between two bundles. When an instruction would not fit in "
"the space remaining in the current bundle, that space is filled with no-op "
"instructions so the instruction starts at the beginning of the next bundle."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:48
msgid ""
"Enabling bundle mode also raises the alignment of every text section that "
"receives instructions to at least the bundle size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:51
msgid ""
"Once enabled, bundle mode stays in effect for the rest of the file and its "
"bundle size is fixed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:55
msgid "``.bundle_lock`` and ``.bundle_unlock``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:63
msgid ""
"A ``.bundle_lock`` / ``.bundle_unlock`` pair encloses a sequence of "
"instructions that must all be placed in a single bundle. The assembler "
"inserts padding before the sequence, if necessary, so that the entire group "
"lands within one bundle rather than straddling a boundary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:68
msgid ""
"The enclosed sequence must fit within a single bundle -- it is an error if "
"the total size of the locked instructions exceeds the bundle size."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:71
msgid ""
"Both directives are only valid in an executable section after bundle mode "
"has been enabled with ``.bundle_align_mode``. A ``.bundle_unlock`` must be "
"matched by a preceding ``.bundle_lock``, and a section may not be switched "
"while a ``.bundle_lock`` is open. A group may not end in a bare instruction "
"prefix, which the assembler could not keep attached to the instruction it "
"modifies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:78
msgid "``align_to_end``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:80
msgid ""
"By default a locked group is padded at the front so that it starts far "
"enough into the bundle to fit. With the ``align_to_end`` option the group is "
"instead padded so that its last instruction ends exactly on a bundle "
"boundary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:85
msgid "Padding"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:87
msgid ""
"By default, bundle padding is emitted as no-op instructions, and neither an "
"instruction nor a padding no-op is ever allowed to cross a bundle boundary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:91
msgid "Prefix padding (x86)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:93
msgid ""
"On x86 the assembler can instead absorb some of the required padding into "
"neighboring instructions by prepending otherwise-ignored instruction "
"prefixes to them, avoiding standalone no-ops. This is controlled by:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:99
msgid ""
"Maximum number of prefixes the assembler may add to an instruction for "
"padding. ``0`` (the default) disables prefix padding, so only no-op "
"instructions are used."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../AlignedBundling.rst:103
msgid ""
"Padding is only ever traded for instruction bytes within the bundle that "
"holds it, so this cannot move an instruction or a locked group across a "
"boundary. It does change where labels land, so it may break assumptions "
"about labels corresponding to particular instructions."
msgstr ""
